{"hash":"29ecbfbcabe298ae","voice":"en-IN-NeerjaExpressiveNeural","rate":"+4%","dur":109.698,"said":"You have come down to Shri Nageshwar. Look at the linga, low and dark, beneath the silver serpent whose hoods spread above it. Pilgrims are told it is of Dwarka stone, with small marks like chakras. Behind him sits Parvati, as Nageshwari. This is the Lord who heard a merchant’s prayer in chains, and rose from the earth to answer it. Fold your hands. The eighth verse of the Dwadasha Jyotirlinga Stotram, traditionally attributed to Adi Shankaracharya, sings: ‘Yamye sadange nagare’tiramye vibhushitangam vividhaishcha bhogaih, sadbhaktimuktipradam isham ekam Shri Naganatham sharanam prapadye.’ ‘In the south, in the lovely town of Sadanga, his form adorned with many splendours, the one Lord who grants true devotion and liberation: in Shri Naganatha I take refuge.’ The shorter verse of the twelve names him ‘Nagesham Darukavane’, Nagesha in the forest of Daruka. Devotees honour him in more than one place: here near Dwarka, by the western sea; at Aundha Nagnath in Maharashtra; and at Jageshwar in the Kumaon hills. Each is dear to its pilgrims, and the Lord is one. Nageshwar is the eighth Jyotirlinga of the Stotram. Pilgrims may visit the twelve Jyotirlingas in any order their journey allows; by tradition, the yatra begins at Somnath, the first, and is held complete with darshan at Grishneshwar, the twelfth, near Ellora. Now the bells ring and the lamps rise. Let the aarti begin. Om Namah Shivaya.\n\nDid you know? Here the sanctum lies below the level of the hall, so pilgrims step down for darshan, as at Mahakaleshwar in Ujjain.","segments":[{"key":"text","start":0,"end":1411},{"key":"didYouKnow","start":1427,"end":1543}],"words":[[0.098,0.168,0,3],[0.278,0.216,4,8],[0.507,0.288,9,13],[0.807,0.264,14,18],[1.084,0.096,19,21],[1.192,0.252,22,26],[1.456,0.721,27,36],[3.101,0.24,38,42],[3.353,0.132,43,45],[3.498,0.084,46,49],[3.594,0.457,50,55],[4.195,0.3,57,60],[4.507,0.216,61,6